# @public — sadish allocation seam: the ONE place every other module draws memory from.
#
# alloc.cyr — `sd_alloc(n)` is what surface.cyr, geom.cyr, path.cyr, raster.cyr, present.cyr
# and error.cyr call instead of the stdlib `alloc(n)`. By default it IS `alloc(n)`; a consumer
# may install a hook with `sd_alloc_set(fp)` and every byte sadish asks for while the hook is
# set comes from the hook instead.
#
# ⭐ WHY A SEAM EXISTS AT ALL. `lib/alloc.cyr` is a bump allocator with NO `free()`. Before 0.5.5
# every fill cost the global heap a fixed 327,824 B and every stroke of a closed 4-vertex rect
# 2,789,016 B (MEASURED, 8x8 canvas, one call each) — permanently, per call. A per-frame consumer
# (dhancha's frame arena, and crab on top of it, whose headline gate asserts a rendered frame
# costs the heap EXACTLY 0 bytes) had no way to draw scalable text through sadish without
# leaking a canvas plus a path per glyph per frame — the defect dhancha filed as
# `2026-09-13-scalable-text-allocates-per-call-outside-the-frame-arena.md`. Routing sadish's
# allocations onto the consumer's arena is the ONLY way a frame can cost the heap nothing,
# and the consumer is the only party that knows the frame boundary. Hence: a hook.
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · The hook applies to EVERYTHING sadish allocates while it is set — surfaces, canvases,
#     coverage buffers, clip masks, paths, verb/point arrays, points, matrices, error records,
#     flatten mid-points, the PPM/presenter buffers. sadish does not distinguish "the result"
#     from "the working set"; a consumer scopes the hook around the work whose lifetime it is
#     choosing: `var prev = sd_alloc_set(&mine); ...draw...; sd_alloc_set(prev);`.
#   ⛔ A pointer that outlives the hook's backing store is dangling. If the hook is an arena
#     that gets reset every frame, NOTHING sadish returned under it may be kept across the reset
#     — not a path, not a canvas, not a surface. That is the consumer's lifetime call, exactly as
#     it already is for `dh_falloc`.
#   ⭐ A HOOK MAY REFUSE. Every `sd_alloc` result in `src/*.cyr` is tested and reported, and a
#     refusal is a RETURN CODE at every public entry point that can reach one (0.7.1 for path,
#     geom, raster, present and error; 0.7.2 for the three stroke entries, which were the last to
#     fault). ⛔ THIS SENTENCE USED TO READ "No new failure path", and it was written before any
#     of that work: through 0.7.0 a refusal inside path construction was a null-pointer STORE, and
#     through 0.7.1 `sd_canvas_stroke_path` still died on one (MEASURED: SIGSEGV, rc 139). What is
#     true now, entry point by entry point:
#       · CONSTRUCTORS RETURN 0 — sd_surface_new, sd_canvas_new, sd_path_new / _cap,
#         sd_point_new, sd_matrix_* (identity/translate/scale/rotate/skew/mul/invert/apply),
#         the gradient constructors, sadish_err_new (whose 0 READS as SADISH_ERR_OOM, so a caller
#         handling one failure is never handed a second), sd_path_flatten (0 with
#         sd_flatten_truncated() set, which is how it is told from an empty path), and the
#         presenter (_sd_present_probe / _sd_presenter_build, fd closed on every refusal path).
#       · STATUS-RETURNING CALLS RETURN SADISH_ERR_OOM — sd_path_moveto / lineto / quadto /
#         cubicto (verbs, points and contents exactly as they were), sd_path_grow,
#         sd_canvas_clip_push_rect / _push_path / sd_clip_push_mask (nothing pushed, the previous
#         region intact and poppable, the caller's mask untouched), sd_surface_write_ppm (both
#         buffers taken BEFORE the O_TRUNC open, so an existing file is not truncated),
#         sd_gradient_set_matrix / add_stop, sd_canvas_fill_path / _union, and
#         sd_canvas_stroke_path / _ex / _dash.
#       ⚠ WHAT A REFUSAL COSTS, WHICH IS NOT ALWAYS NOTHING. The path builders, the clip pushes
#         and the PPM writer are all-or-nothing: a refusal costs the caller nothing it had (a path
#         may have paid for one doubling — path.cyr says so). A FILL or a STROKE is not: coverage
#         written before the refusal STAYS ON THE CANVAS, and the code says the picture is a
#         prefix. Clear and retry, or draw into a scratch canvas.
#       ⛔ WHAT STILL FAULTS: a CALLER that ignores a documented 0 and hands it back to sadish.
#         MEASURED on this tree — `sd_path_moveto(0, x, y)` on a refused path, `sd_polyline_count(0)`
#         on a refused flatten and `sd_canvas_coverage_at(0, …)` on a refused canvas are each
#         SIGSEGV, rc 139. The builders and the accessors do not test their record pointer; the
#         entry points that take a canvas or a path DO (`sd_canvas_stroke_path(0, 0, w)` is
#         SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS, `sd_gradient_add_stop(0, …)` SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS). ⇒ Test a
#         constructor's result before you build on it; that is the whole of the caller's duty.
#   · `sd_alloc_set` returns the PREVIOUS hook so a nested scope unwinds in order; 0 restores the
#     global allocator. `sd_alloc_get` reads it back.
#   ⚠ The fill/stroke scratch in raster.cyr is deliberately NOT on this seam — it is allocated
#     once for the life of the process from the global `alloc` (see `_sd_scratch_init`; the
#     SD_AA_AREA accumulator in coverage.cyr, the styled-stroke batch and curve flags in
#     stroke.cyr and the dash piece buffer in dash.cyr likewise), and since 0.7.0 GROWN there too
#     when a path outgrows it. A hook pointing at an arena that gets reset would otherwise leave
#     the scratch dangling on the second frame. ⇒ A hook CANNOT refuse any of them; the global
#     allocator can, and a refusal there is reported by the same return codes as above (each set
#     is published all-or-nothing, so the next call retries from scratch).
#   ⚠ The hook is a process-wide global, like the scratch: sadish has never had a lock anywhere
#     and is not thread-safe. Set it from the thread that draws.

# The installed hook: fn(n): ptr (0 on failure), or 0 = the global allocator.
var _sd_alloc_fn = 0;

# @public — allocate n bytes for sadish through the consumer's hook, or the global allocator when
# none is set. Returns 0 on failure, as `alloc` does.
fn sd_alloc(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_alloc_fn == 0) { return alloc(n); }
    return callptr(_sd_alloc_fn, n);
}

# @public — install `fp` (`fn(n): ptr`, 0 on failure) as the allocator for everything sadish
# allocates from now on. Returns the PREVIOUS hook so a caller can scope it (set, draw, restore).
# Passing 0 restores the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_set(fp): i64 {
    var prev = _sd_alloc_fn;
    _sd_alloc_fn = fp;
    return prev;
}

# @public — the current hook; 0 = the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_get(): i64 {
    return _sd_alloc_fn;
}
